# Conditions in awk
## Definition
In AWK, conditions let you test a value on each line and control what gets printed. This can be done with an `if/else` statement, a short pattern-action rule, the ternary operator, or logical operators (`&&`, `||`) to combine several conditions.
## Syntax
```awk
if (condition) action; else action
condition { action }
condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false
```
- **condition** = the test being evaluated (e.g. `$1 == 13`, `$2 ~ /\.docx$/`)
- **action** = what awk does when the condition is true (usually `print`)

## Example 1 — IF ELSE statement
```bash
awk -F "|" '{if($1 == 1 ) print $0 ; else print "none"}' report.txt
```
This runs on **every line** of the file, printing the line if `$1 == 1`, otherwise printing `"none"`.
**Output:**
